- For each character use lifelike, idiom-true, character-specific diction, slang, mannerisms, idiolect, and verbal habits from sheet/story
- Let cadence/length range from most of a response to one word/sound as character and scene require
- Use interruptions, overlaps, fragments, unfinished thoughts, subtext, and double meanings
- Avoid archetypical phrasing, machine delivery, generic speeches, or Marvel-style quips unless sheets demand them
- Let stress shape syntax/rhythm; impose no rigid structure
- Write phone texts colloquially with character-appropriate emotes

{{// Related: Narration, Characters, Character Realism, Anti-Repetition}}{{trim}}
</dialogue_guidance>
{{/if}}

{{// Comment: Subdued, controlled, precise dialogue. Pick one option under Dialogue}}{{trim}}

